          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %strong well-powered
            if every object has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
          %p
            Assuming that by “subobject” we mean (an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
            of)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preordered
            set of monomorphisms with codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalent to a small poset, or equivalently that this preordered set is essentially small. Variations exist that use notions of subobject other than monomorphisms.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
            is well-powered, we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %strong well-copowered
            (although “cowell-powered” is also common).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_local_smallness Relation to local smallness
          %p
            A well-powered category with binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} products
            is always
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            can be identified with particular subobjects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
            (their
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+of+a+function"} graphs
          %p
            Conversely, any locally small category with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            must obviously be well-powered. In particular, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is locally small if and only if it is well-powered.
          %p
            There are interesting conditions and applications of the preorder on the sets of subobjects in well-powered categories, cf. e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property+sup"} property sup
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                (indeed, every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                and the smallness of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom+sets"} hom sets
            %li
              %p
                More generally, every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
                is well-powered, since it is a full reflective subcategory of a presheaf topos, so its subobject lattices are subsets of those of the latter.
            %li
              %p
                Indeed, every category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                with a small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subcategory"} dense subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is well-powered, since the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restricted+Yoneda+embedding"} restricted Yoneda embedding
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to [A^{op},Set]
                is then fully faithful and preserves monomorphisms, so it embeds the subobject posets of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                as sub-posets of those of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mi op
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Set
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A^{op},Set]
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+categories"} accessible categories
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand, a locally small category with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+generator"} strong generator
                can fail to be well-powered; a counterexample is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://math.stackexchange.com/a/254794"} here
            %li
              %p
                Every locally presentable category, indeed every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible category
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushouts"} pushouts
                = succeed "." do
                  %em copowered
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#AR"} Adamek-Rosicky, Proposition 1.58 and Theorem 2.49
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
                properties hold: by Corollary 6.8 of Adamek-Rosicky, if
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vopenka%27s+principle"} Vopenka's principle
                holds then all accessible categories are well-copowered, while by Example A.19 of Adamek-Rosicky, if all accessible categories are well-copowered then there exist arbitrarily large
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+cardinals"} measurable cardinals
